Z||000002||AusFiero||10-14-2001||10:31 AM||||The steering stabiliser is the first casualty of a right hand drive conversion. Works fine without one.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero2.gif">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.com.au" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||10-14-2001||10:48 AM||||same here, just something else unnecessary thatll break. I thru them away on both my cars years ago. no diff.<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52990885"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fformula88||10-14-2001||11:32 AM||||I dunno if I agree... when my father replaced the one on his old 87 coupe after 110,000 miles the difference was like night and day in the cars steering and handling!  The car was very twitchy before, much more linear after.  It was just a lot easier to pick a line and stick to it.  I would recommend replacing it if its old!  I think we paid like $20 or $30 for it at Napa.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Master_Sushi||10-14-2001||11:47 AM||||haha, the one in my 84 coupe is rusted out with big holes in it. So I shouldnt bother replaceing it then? I was thinking of buying a new one but if it dosent do anything I wont!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||10-14-2001||04:00 PM||||The stabilizers are available thru many parts stores but you may have to order it.<P>Many new ones don't have anything to protect the piston rod. Monroe, I think, hase boots in multiple colors for them. You definatly want the boot to protect the rod or the stabilizer may not last long. It's exposed to allot of trash there.<P>The stabilizer isn't required but a good on can make a difference. I haven't had the money to replace mine yet. you can look them up in Partsamerica.com autozone.com and others. Keep in mind that most of what NAPA lists are rebranded Monroe parts. NAPA even licensed use of the Sense a Track name.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tina||10-14-2001||10:30 PM||||Anybody have a part # for it handy?<P>Tina  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Master_Sushi||10-14-2001||11:02 PM||||What does it do though? Does it make the stearing wheel shake less? Or does it not allow for sudden turns, like if you hit a big pot hole? Sorry I have no idea!||||reg||5
Z||000009||Master_Sushi||10-15-2001||11:38 AM||||bump...<P>Help! Whats the "Steering stablizer" do???<BR>I couldn't find much on it, besides that most people get rid of it... Should I just can mine?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Fformula88||10-15-2001||07:02 PM||||I wouldn't can it.  GM didn't design Fieros to waste money on putting extra parts in them.  They paid engineers good money to design systems that work well with all their parts.   So I'd say that it does indeed have a purpose and makes a difference somewhere.<P>As I said, the steering felt less gittery with the new one vs the old worn out one (like yours, it was all rusted away too!).  I do not know exactly what it is there for, but the car definately steered and handled better with it than without.  ||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||10-15-2001||08:17 PM||||The Monroe ones are only about $30 at partsamerica.com and they are listed with steering parts. the boot is another $5 and is listed in shock/strut accessories.<P>The boots come in Yellow, Green, Orange, Pink, White, Black, Red, and Blue.<P>The main thing about the stabilizers in cars is the same as with off road trucks. It absorbes impact from rough road, rail crossings, etc.<P>One that is working right isn't noticable most of the time and can make a significant reduction in driver fatigue. One that's missing/broken means allot more impact up the steering gear when you are on something rough.<P>The Stabilizers are generally designed along the same lines as velocity sensitive shocks/struts. They move smoothly and fairly easily when they move slow but tighten considerably in responce to a good bump.||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||10-15-2001||08:34 PM||||what is does is act like a shock absorber. If you hit like a pothole, its supposed to slow down any quick movement of the steering wheel. It still allows you to turn the wheels, but puts resistance on it so it cant be done quick. I like it being quick for 'sporty' driving so I took them off my Fiero and Mercedes SL. Compare it to running on foot on pavement or in mud.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Master_Sushi||10-15-2001||09:54 PM||||hmmmm....<BR>It sound like something that would be nice to have. I might put in a new one. This is just on my winter beater 84 though. Basicly I drive to and from school/work in it a few times a day. I do drive on some crappy roads, so I think I will probably replace it.<P>Thank ya!||||reg||1
